1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,360
Welcome to the second episode of Earthlings 101.

2
00:00:02,852 --> 00:00:05,375
Today you will learn about the origin of life on earth.

3
00:00:05,741 --> 00:00:09,056
Most earthlings think their life originated in the oceans of young earth.

4
00:00:09,444 --> 00:00:13,601
But actually, it came from space - on a stray missile from the Squid War.

5
00:00:48,385 --> 00:00:54,568
The missile that plunged into the oceans of young earth didn't contain any explosive, nuclear or quantum collapse charge.

6
00:00:54,568 --> 00:01:02,324
Its payload consisted of two million specimen of a simple, carbon-based, self-replicating nano weapon, a primitive variant of the dreaded grey goo.

7
00:01:02,786 --> 00:01:09,806
By the way, earthlings would call this a bio-weapon, or artificial life form, but only because life on earth has developed from this weapon.

8
00:01:09,987 --> 00:01:12,898
You may remember the Squid War from early history class.

9
00:01:13,370 --> 00:01:20,313
It was a merciless war between the then Andromeda Dominion and the old Squid Kingdom, fought with all means authorized by the central bureaucracy.

10
00:01:20,313 --> 00:01:31,833
The Dominion eventually crushed the Squids, but their victory application was rejected by the bureaucracy because they couldn't account for one of the three million planetary missiles fired in this war: The very missile that had found its way to earth.

11
00:01:32,013 --> 00:01:33,758
The bio-weapon was based on carbon.

12
00:01:34,361 --> 00:01:40,407
Most nanolabs use carbon to build artificial life forms, simply because carbon atoms are ideal building blocks.

13
00:01:40,912 --> 00:01:46,083
See, the bonds an atom can make is defined by the electrons and free slots in their outer electron shell.

14
00:01:46,083 --> 00:01:52,525
A covalent bond is an electron-sharing-deal between two atoms : I put an electron in your slot, you put an electron into mine.

15
00:01:53,047 --> 00:01:58,912
So, Fluorine can make one bond, oxygen can make two, nitrogen can make three, carbon can make four.

16
00:01:59,812 --> 00:02:06,152
Boron has five slots but only three electrons to share, so it can make three bonds, Beryllium can make two, and Lithium one.

17
00:02:06,475 --> 00:02:15,736
That's why carbon is the nanotechnicians' best friend: With no less than 4 connections per atom you can build a lot of things: chains, grids, rings, - you name it.

18
00:02:15,876 --> 00:02:25,935
With some other atoms like oxygen or nitrogen here and there, and the free slots plugged with tiny hydrogen atoms, building nano machines is as easy as playing with construction kits for young aliens.

19
00:02:25,935 --> 00:02:31,035
So, back to our nano weapon which has just been released in the warm oceans of young earth.

20
00:02:31,415 --> 00:02:39,405
Those weapons work like viral religions: A viral religion consists basically of a set of instructions to make the believers spread the religion, which may or may not involve killing non-believers.

21
00:02:41,647 --> 00:02:45,627
Self-replicating nano weapons work the same way - only without the believers.

22
00:02:45,627 --> 00:02:53,667
The instructions come usually with some molecular nanomachines called enzymes which perform the actual replication, and a wrapping to hold it all together.

23
00:02:54,067 --> 00:03:08,088
In this case, the instructions were written on a molecule called DNA, it's basically a primitive variant of XNA, the trademark molecule of the Arctarian nanolabs, which is not surprising as the Arctarian weapon factories supplied both sides during the Squid War.

24
00:03:08,088 --> 00:03:22,019
The DNA of these ancient bio-weapons contained usually instructions for self-replication, plus an actual message which was often an insult, like, "Eat this, tentacle heads!" or "Fisheaters must die!", or in this case, "Squids are stupid".

25
00:03:22,579 --> 00:03:26,039
So, those little bags of DNA and enzymes started self-replicating.

26
00:03:27,739 --> 00:03:31,653
Unfortunately, replicating isn't of much use if you don't have a source for new bio-mass.

27
00:03:31,653 --> 00:03:38,993
The creature was designed to replicate on a squid planet which was rich in elementary carbon, so biomass wasn't supposed to be an issue.

28
00:03:39,373 --> 00:03:42,173
But here, on earth, elementary carbon was rare.

29
00:03:42,533 --> 00:03:45,848
That's where the creature started to evolve - the beginning of life on earth.

30
00:03:46,474 --> 00:03:50,872
Let me get this straight: life on earth is wholly different from life anywhere else.

31
00:03:51,035 --> 00:03:56,325
In the whole Galaxy, the evolution of life is regulated by the all-mighty and omnipresent Galactic Bureaucracy.

32
00:03:56,325 --> 00:04:05,175
When a planet is approved for developing life, it is put under the responsibility of an approved bio-administrator who comes from an advanced civilization of class 14 or higher.

33
00:04:05,356 --> 00:04:20,688
The administrator supervises evolution, decides on creation of new species, approves mutations, oversees the development of civilisation and, most important of all, does all the administrative steps required by the galactic bureau of planetary evolution, which means quite a lot of paperwork.

34
00:04:20,688 --> 00:04:27,488
The official goal of all this is to create sentient life and make it reach the point where it develops the concept of money so that it can pay taxes.

35
00:04:28,448 --> 00:04:36,913
But the real goal of every bio-administrator is to push the lifeforms on the planet to develop scripture, from which point on they can do their own paperwork and the administrator can leave the planet.

36
00:04:38,276 --> 00:04:43,192
However, as mentioned in the last episode, earth is out of reach of the galactic bureaucracy.

37
00:04:43,555 --> 00:04:50,941
In consequence, life on earth follows a completely unregulated, wild evolution, an explosion of life limited only by natural resources.

38
00:04:51,543 --> 00:04:56,151
In one word, any bio-anarchists wet dream, and any bio-administrator's nightmare.

39
00:04:56,417 --> 00:05:01,917
This is, by the way, the reason why the Dominion never officially admitted that they lost their missile on earth.

40
00:05:02,677 --> 00:05:07,676
Uncontrolled evolution is based on three mechanisms: replication, mutation and natural selection.

41
00:05:08,321 --> 00:05:12,063
The simplest form of replication is duplicating the code and the individual.

42
00:05:12,548 --> 00:05:17,130
There is another form of replication called sexual reproduction, which we will discuss later.

43
00:05:17,490 --> 00:05:19,005
Mutation is often misunderstood.

44
00:05:19,462 --> 00:05:24,362
It doesn't consist in having a tentacle growing out of one's head after receiving a bit too much radiation.

45
00:05:24,662 --> 00:05:29,128
That may be the case for Tangerian Metamutants, but it's not how it works on earth.

46
00:05:29,128 --> 00:05:38,129
On earth, mutation means a usually small variation in the offspring due to damaged genetic code - say, a different skin color, or stronger leg muscles.

47
00:05:38,591 --> 00:05:44,855
As for natural selection, it means that nature favours individuals which are good at feeding, reproducing and not dying.

48
00:05:45,276 --> 00:05:48,184
Humans usually paraphrase this as "survival of the fittest" .

49
00:05:48,611 --> 00:05:53,579
This might be correct, as long as survival and fitness serve the ultimate purpose: Spreading the code.

50
00:05:53,579 --> 00:05:57,919
The code doesn't care about the well-being of the individual, as long as it is spread.

51
00:05:58,339 --> 00:06:02,270
If this means being eaten by your offspring, evolution is fine with it.

52
00:06:02,413 --> 00:06:05,694
A simple example of natural selection would be an animal running from a predator.

53
00:06:05,981 --> 00:06:09,741
If by chance it has grown a fifth leg allowing to outrun the predator - great.

54
00:06:10,057 --> 00:06:15,256
But actually, it is enough - and far more probable - to run just a little bit faster than one's sibling.

55
00:06:15,256 --> 00:06:19,699
This way, even small variations may make the difference between dying and surviving.

56
00:06:20,179 --> 00:06:24,533
With time, these variations will accumulate and the species will become faster and faster.

57
00:06:24,876 --> 00:06:26,576
But back to our little organism.

58
00:06:26,996 --> 00:06:32,556
It needed carbon to multiply, but carbon was only available in form of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere.

59
00:06:33,296 --> 00:06:41,004
Besides this, there were pretty useless nitrogen, and tons of hydric acid - you know, this disgusting liquid which covers two thirds of the planet.

60
00:06:41,004 --> 00:06:46,914
So, evolution kicked in and our organisms developed a method to transform carbon dioxide into bio-mass.

61
00:06:47,335 --> 00:06:50,895
In its evolved form, this process is called photosynthesis.

62
00:06:51,535 --> 00:06:58,434
Photosynthesis consists basically in using sunlight to transform carbon dioxide and hydric acid into oxygen and a chemical called sugar.

63
00:06:58,847 --> 00:07:08,593
Oxygen is a toxic substance which had to be released into the atmosphere, but sugar is pretty useful : It comes in different variants and can be used for short-term energy storage and to obtain bio-mass.

64
00:07:08,593 --> 00:07:15,540
You can also use it to build chains to obtain starch for long-term energy storage, or cellulose for structural reinforcement.

65
00:07:16,241 --> 00:07:19,354
Photosynthesis is still used by plants on earth to build their bio-mass.

66
00:07:19,866 --> 00:07:29,106
It's noteworthy that only one fifteenth of the sugar created by photosynthesis comes from the hydric acid - actually, only the tiny hydrogen atoms used to plug the free slots.

67
00:07:29,406 --> 00:07:31,446
The rest comes from carbon dioxide.

68
00:07:31,446 --> 00:07:38,963
So, when you visit earth and find gigantic forests made of plants, remember that 93% of their dry matter is made of "air".

69
00:07:39,224 --> 00:07:45,413
Now that our little organism knew how to literally create bio-mass out of thin air, it multiplied and took over the whole planet.

70
00:07:45,673 --> 00:07:59,953
While doing so, it evolved into a variety of species, some of which developed a number of improvements, for example a cell core, tiny tentacles to move around, or sexual reproduction - basically mixing the codes of two individuals to create a new one.

71
00:08:00,333 --> 00:08:03,404
We will learn more about sexual reproduction in the next episode.

72
00:08:03,688 --> 00:08:05,996
Everything seemed fine for our little light-eating organisms.

73
00:08:06,670 --> 00:08:08,881
But after a while, a problem came up.

74
00:08:09,290 --> 00:08:12,141
Remember oxygen, this toxic by product of photosynthesis ?

75
00:08:12,859 --> 00:08:17,187
This stuff had accumulated in the atmosphere and now reached a dangerous concentration.

76
00:08:17,509 --> 00:08:26,588
This could have been the end of life on earth - but luckily, one organism mutated and found a way of transforming oxygen and sugar back into carbon dioxide, hydric acid and energy.

77
00:08:26,809 --> 00:08:30,634
This is called respiration, and it's basically photosynthesis backwards.

78
00:08:31,017 --> 00:08:42,957
Now we had two kinds of creatures: Light eaters - also known as plants - who create sugar and oxygen, and oxygen breathers, also called animals, who transform sugar and oxygen back into carbon dioxide.

79
00:08:43,617 --> 00:08:45,335
But how did the animals get the sugar ?

80
00:08:45,737 --> 00:08:47,569
Well, by devouring plants!

81
00:08:47,813 --> 00:08:51,018
Soon those animals multiplied and lowered the oxygen level in the atmosphere.

82
00:08:51,691 --> 00:08:56,784
Since then, plants and animals share the planet, and each group produces the gas the other group needs.

83
00:08:57,048 --> 00:08:58,625
But evolution didn't stop there.

84
00:08:58,956 --> 00:09:04,209
With time, it grew creatures which were better and better at feeding, reproducing and not dying.

85
00:09:04,209 --> 00:09:09,360
The light-eating cells became multicellular plants, conquered the oceans and then the land .

86
00:09:09,826 --> 00:09:17,463
The oxygen-breathers evolved into multicellular animals, and developed traits like symmetry, a neural system and an inclination for moving around.

87
00:09:18,007 --> 00:09:21,625
One branch evolved into small creatures with an exoskeleton called insects.

88
00:09:22,368 --> 00:09:27,220
Others developed an endoskeleton, eyes and a mouth and eventually crawled onto land and grew legs.

89
00:09:27,705 --> 00:09:31,815
Most species of this time are extinct by now - except for, the microbes.

90
00:09:34,779 --> 00:09:35,742
Strategic advice!

91
00:09:36,098 --> 00:09:42,021
If you want to extinguish all humans and animals on earth to prepare it for colonization, don't forget the insects!

92
00:09:42,421 --> 00:09:46,617
They are everywhere, they are hard to kill, and they can be really, really annoying.

93
00:09:47,225 --> 00:09:53,195
The most obvious difference between plants and animals is that animals move around, whereas plants stay at one place.

94
00:09:53,195 --> 00:09:54,775
Why is this so?

95
00:09:55,155 --> 00:10:04,106
Well, animals have to seek edible plants, or run after other animals, whereas plants feed on air, hydric acid and sunlight, resources which are available everywhere.

96
00:10:04,630 --> 00:10:07,811
However, spreading one's DNA isn't easy when you can't move.

97
00:10:08,214 --> 00:10:12,345
So, plants eventually found a way to get animals to carry their DNA around.

98
00:10:12,345 --> 00:10:15,377
Remember sugar, the main product of photosynthesis?

99
00:10:15,758 --> 00:10:19,245
Animals love that stuff, as it's easy to process and rich in energy.

100
00:10:19,646 --> 00:10:23,836
Normally, plants don't store large amounts of sugar because they don't want to get eaten.

101
00:10:24,058 --> 00:10:32,227
But now, they developed exposed sugar-filled sexual organs called flowers to bribe insects into carrying their DNA to other plants for sexual reproduction.

102
00:10:32,227 --> 00:10:38,085
Then they put their seed into delicious sugar packages called fruits to bribe bigger animals into carrying their seed around.

103
00:10:38,991 --> 00:10:42,291
Both fruits and flowers are colored to attract the attention of animals.

104
00:10:42,751 --> 00:10:46,230
In the language of plants, bright colors mean "Here is sugar".

105
00:10:46,698 --> 00:10:56,838
Animals, on the other side, don't want parts of their body to be eaten, so in the language of animals, bright colors either mean "I'm poisonous", or, "Catch me if you can, I have wings!".

106
00:10:57,818 --> 00:11:05,686
All those plants, insects, fruit-eaters, predators and microbes form a complex, self regulating system, without any need of a bio-administrator.

107
00:11:06,954 --> 00:11:08,647
Earthlings call this an ecosystem.

108
00:11:09,321 --> 00:11:10,345
Scientific advice.

109
00:11:10,686 --> 00:11:18,631
A popular experiment among students consists in abducting an earthling, a cellulose-eating animal and some plants and putting them into a tank in order to create a micro-ecosystem.

110
00:11:19,892 --> 00:11:20,992
But this doesn't work.

111
00:11:21,399 --> 00:11:26,672
You get much better results when you put some ocean plants and animals into a small tank filled with hydric acid.

112
00:11:26,672 --> 00:11:33,312
At some point, evolution led to bigger and bigger animals, until the planet was ruled by giant creatures called dinosaurs.

113
00:11:33,938 --> 00:11:37,206
Some of them evolved into small flying creatures called birds.

114
00:11:37,473 --> 00:11:41,763
Other creatures developed the ability of nourishing their offspring with a liquid called milk.

115
00:11:42,244 --> 00:11:48,964
One branch of these animals acquired some intelligence and dexterity in manipulating objects, and eventually evolved into earthlings.

116
00:11:48,964 --> 00:11:57,964
The first earthlings lived pretty much like animals, collecting fruits, hunting animals and protecting from predators - except that they developed their own tools to do so.

117
00:11:58,324 --> 00:12:01,462
This is where natural evolution ends and civilization begins.

118
00:12:01,872 --> 00:12:03,084
Tips for tourists.

119
00:12:03,467 --> 00:12:08,777
If you are a time-traveller and you visit the time of the dinosaurs, please don't take any dinosaurs with you.

120
00:12:08,777 --> 00:12:14,937
If it wasn't for countless time tourists taking animals away as souvenirs or pets, dinosaurs wouldn't be extinct.

121
00:12:16,823 --> 00:12:20,398
There is a small group of earthlings who might not have developed by evolution.

122
00:12:20,743 --> 00:12:22,663
They call themselves creationists.

123
00:12:23,123 --> 00:12:29,460
According to their own claims, they have been created by an extraterrestrial bio-engineer some thousands of solar cycles ago.

124
00:12:29,460 --> 00:12:35,684
His intervention triggered an environmental catastrophe, an inundation with hydric acid which killed all other life in the area.

125
00:12:35,886 --> 00:12:45,072
The creationists, warned by their creator, saved themselves onto a ship, crossed an ocean and settled in a place called America, where they are waiting for their creator to pick them up.

126
00:12:45,492 --> 00:12:49,639
However, the whole story seems unlikely and doesn't fit the bureaucratic records.

127
00:12:49,941 --> 00:12:54,425
So most alien scientists agree that this story has probably been made up by earthlings.

128
00:12:54,912 --> 00:12:57,849
In this episode, we have seen the evolution that led to earthlings.

129
00:12:58,457 --> 00:13:17,197
This evolution was driven by one single force : An ancient code, programmed to replicate itself at infinitum, unleashed in an uncontrolled environment, pursuing its goal to the point of conquering a whole planet, of creating intelligent life and of building a spacefaring civilization - and all this in order to deliver one simple message : Squids are stupid.

130
00:13:17,997 --> 00:13:26,610
In the next episode we will learn more about sexuality : How it works, how it alters the rules of evolution, and why it wouldn't even exist if it wasn't for : parasites.

